; generated-format instantiation 2026-08-08; re-carved per D12 same day —
; location = a containment predicate over a designated container, so it is a
; DESCRIPTION (the contained-in claim), while the coordinate (the container's
; name) is a designator. The old peer-of-designator/description position was
; the partition-breaker.
|term[location]
  :status settled
  :subject-field addressing
  :layer framing-vocabulary
  :gloss A containment predicate over a designated container — the owned description: the container's community can enumerate and route it.
  :see-also [@containment-coordinate @path @origin]
  |designation location
    :status preferred
    :channel prose
  |definition
    :defined-by @def-locations-and-paths
    :anchor identity-vs-location
    :projection paraphrase
    A location is a containment predicate over a designated container: the coordinate names the container (a designator); the contained-in claim it makes is a description, distinguished from free predicates by subset-composability, delegable resolution, and partial policing.
  |formal
    :notation <latex:loc_c(x) := x \sqsubseteq c, \; c \; designated>
    :reading The predicate is over containment in a NAMED container; chains of these compose by subset.
  |set-rel :kind subset-of :to @description
  |rel :kind names-via :to @containment-coordinate :card +
  |invariant
    Distinguished from free descriptions by: subset-composability (location chains conjoin by nesting); delegable resolution (the owned description — the container's community can enumerate and route); partial policing.
  |confused
    :with @path
    A location describes where a thing sits; a path is steps between locations — they interchange safely only in the universal-path case (D11).
  |note :audience maintainer
    "Standing alone, a location is a scope" — mint of scope proposed, NOT ratified (D12 session); do not cite scope as a term yet.
